The bedside picture is a right sided systolic ejection murmur in the pulmonary area.

A harsh systolic ejection murmur maximal at the left upper 2nd-3rd intercostal space / left upper sternal border, radiating toward the left clavicle/neck, increased with inspiration and associated with a palpable thrill is the classic pulmonary area ejection murmur. Inspiration augments right sided filling and therefore augments any right sided ejection murmur. Right ventricular hypertrophy on the ECG and a history of faint childhood cyanosis with now progressive exertional dyspnoea, fatigue and lightheadedness is chronic right heart volume/pressure overload.

Strictly speaking that acoustic picture is isolated pulmonic valvular stenosis, which is not listed. Pulmonic stenosis is not offered as a choice.

Of the listed lesions the only one that can reproduce a pulmonary area systolic ejection/flow murmur that is louder with inspiration and is accompanied by RV hypertrophy is increased pulmonary flow from a left-to-right atrial shunt.

A large left-to-right shunt at atrial level gives a pulmonary flow / functional pulmonary stenosis systolic ejection murmur at the LUSB, radiating to the clavicle, louder with inspiration and often with a thrill when flow is high. Chronic pulmonary overcirculation gives exertional dyspnoea, fatigue, lightheadedness and ECG RVH.

The ECG nuance here is important. A classic secundum ASD shows right axis deviation with incomplete RBBB and RVH. The given left axis deviation is discordant with uncomplicated secundum ASD and with isolated pulmonic stenosis and points to an ostium primum / AV canal type ASD, which gives leftward frontal axis / left anterior fascicular block, often first degree AV block, with RV volume overload and a pulmonary flow ejection murmur that can be thrill-like. That is the only way to reconcile left axis deviation + RVH with an atrial septal defect hypothesis.

All other choices are anatomically inconsistent:
Ebstein → holosystolic TR, not LUSB ejection;
PDA → continuous machinery;
MVP → mid/late systolic click at apex;
HCM with obstruction → harsh systolic at left lower sternal border/apex, accentuated by Valsalva;
Tricuspid stenosis → diastolic rumbling at right lower sternal border;
VSD → harsh holosystolic at left lower sternal border.

On a strict anatomic basis the murmur described is pulmonic stenosis and therefore H None of these choices would be strictly correct. If a choice is forced from the list provided, the only lesion that can mimic the LUSB inspiratory accentuated systolic ejection murmur with RVH is an atrial septal defect, and the left axis deviation is best explained by an ostium primum/AV canal type ASD. That is the intended USMLE surrogate.

D. Atrial septal defect, ostium primum type accounting for the left axis deviation.

